MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: edad3068bd72c46f0a544237e3cec695, type: 3}
  m_Name: Chapter_3
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ChapterData
  chapterNumber: 3
  chapterTitle: Packing the Hospital Bag
  timelineText: Third trimester, close to the due date
  storyText: 'You''re thirty-eight weeks pregnant, and the baby could come any day.
    You barely slept again: you woke every hour, the heartburn burned all night,
    and even the stairs leave you out of breath. The hospital bag is still half empty.
    The newborn clothes need washing, the hospital numbers need writing down, and
    dinner won''t cook itself. And the messages keep coming: "Any baby yet?" Pack
    the hospital bag and get the house ready, before it''s too late.'
  weekContext: Week 37, third trimester
  obstacleText: Broken sleep, heartburn and breathlessness
  mainTaskText: Packing the hospital bag and getting the house ready
  contentWarningText: This game explores the emotional and physical strain of pregnancy
    and early parenthood. You can exit at any time.
  showMeterIntro: 0
  showContentWarning: 0
  meterIntroNote: 'Move with the arrow keys or WASD, and press E to use things. While
    packing the hospital bag, hold E and respond to the prompts: hold SPACE to catch
    her breath, tap Q to walk slowly, and tap F to notice the baby''s kicks.'
  musicClip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 61e6a3f285cf84a7a844274acb435ebd, type: 3}
  ambienceClip: {fileID: 0}
  storyVoiceover: {fileID: 8300000, guid: da750e1433ba42c3aa3e7e27f22ee99c, type: 3}
  hourTransitionText: She has to lie down. The bag can wait.
  bodyDrainStory: Broken sleep, heartburn and breathlessness meant every bend and
    lift to pack the bag cost her something.
  mindDrainStory: Lists, hospital numbers and 'any baby yet?' messages piled up on
    a mind running on broken sleep.
  personDrainStory: She was getting everything ready for the baby, with nothing left
    over for herself.
  maskDrainStory: She kept replying 'all good, no baby yet!' and hid how uncomfortable
    and anxious she really was.
  partnerHourSeconds: 120
  baseDrain:
    body: 0.035
    mind: 0.025
    feelingLikeAPerson: 0.025
    mask: 0.02
  choices:
  - label: Stop and rest
    multipliers:
      body: 0.5
      mind: 1.3
      feelingLikeAPerson: 1
      mask: 1
  - label: Keep packing
    multipliers:
      body: 1.6
      mind: 0.6
      feelingLikeAPerson: 1
      mask: 1
  choiceTriggerTaskId: hospital_bag
  mainTaskHitMeter: 0
  mainTaskHitFraction: 0.5
  responses:
  - label: Make something she can eat
    nextChapterEase:
      body: 0.85
      mind: 1
      feelingLikeAPerson: 1
      mask: 1
    impactText: 'A small, plain dinner, early in the evening. The heartburn settles
      before bed, and she gets a few more hours of sleep.

      Next chapter: her
      Body drains more slowly.'
    learnText: Heartburn affects about 44% of pregnant women, and reflux symptoms
      rise from about 26% in the first trimester to about 56% in the third.
    helpText: Smaller meals, no late-night eating and raising the head of the bed
      are the usual first steps. If heartburn keeps waking her, medicines work better
      than diet changes alone, so help her ask her midwife or GP.
    sourceText: Khurmatullina et al. (2026), BMC Pregnancy Childbirth 26:44; Phupong
      & Hanprasertpong (2015), Cochrane Database Syst Rev CD011379
  - label: Help her rest
    nextChapterEase:
      body: 1
      mind: 0.85
      feelingLikeAPerson: 1
      mask: 1
    impactText: 'You set up pillows so she can sleep on her side, and you take the
      evening jobs. She wakes less, and her head is clearer in the morning.

      Next
      chapter: her Mind drains more slowly.'
    learnText: Sleep problems affect around 70% of pregnant women and get worse in
      the third trimester. From 28 weeks, going to sleep on her back was linked with
      2.6 times the odds of late stillbirth compared with her left side.
    helpText: Help her get comfortable on her side with pillows, and encourage naps
      when she can. Take over evening chores so she can go to bed earlier.
    sourceText: Witkowska-Zimny et al. (2024), Int J Womens Health 16:345-71; Cronin
      et al. (2019), EClinicalMedicine 10:49-57
  - label: Plan for after the birth
    nextChapterEase:
      body: 1
      mind: 1
      feelingLikeAPerson: 0.85
      mask: 1
    impactText: 'You agree a visitor plan, freeze some meals and set up a feeding
      spot with water and snacks. One less thing for her to hold in her head.

      Next
      chapter: Feeling like a person drains more slowly.'
    learnText: In a consensus study, perinatal experts recommended that couples work
      out together how they'll divide housework and baby care, as one way to prevent
      depression and anxiety after the birth.
    helpText: Decide now who visits and when, and who will tell people. Batch-cook
      and freeze meals, and agree how you'll share nights and chores, so the planning
      doesn't all fall to her.
    sourceText: Pilkington et al. (2016), BMC Psychiatry 16:23
  - label: Carry on as normal
    nextChapterEase:
      body: 3
      mind: 3
      feelingLikeAPerson: 3
      mask: 3
    impactText: 'She''s up at 3 am with heartburn again, on her own, packing the
      hospital bag in her head.

      Next chapter: all of her meters drain faster.'
    learnText: Sleep problems affect around 70% of pregnant women and get worse in
      the third trimester. Poor sleep after birth is linked with more postnatal depression
      and anxiety.
    helpText: Notice the signs, like pillows piled on the couch or antacids on the
      bench. Ask what kept her up, and take one thing off her list today.
    sourceText: Witkowska-Zimny et al. (2024), Int J Womens Health 16:345-71
  learnHeading: In the third trimester
  selfHourNotice: 'She''s out of breath on the couch: "I''m fine, just big."'
